<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=linnaean-taxonomy">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Linnaean taxonomy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Linnaean classification</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Linnaean taxonomy is the form of biological classification set out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a> (1735) and later works, in which he described three kingdoms, animal, vegetable and mineral, each divided into classes, orders, genera and species. Ranked classification goes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, and Linnaeus neither invented it nor gave it its present form. The first edition of his Species Plantarum (1753) and the tenth edition of Systema Naturae (1758) are accepted as starting points of biological nomenclature, and his binomial names for species take priority over those of others. His classes of plants were based on the number and arrangement of stamens and pistils.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linnaean-taxonomy.jpg" alt="Linnaean taxonomy" width="330" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Linnaeus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linné-Systema_Naturae_1735.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1735</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Ranked classification goes back to Plato and Aristotle, and Linnaeus neither invented the concept nor gave it its present form; 'Linnaean taxonomy' does not exist as a single defined system.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=linnaean-taxonomy">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
